## Part 2: Start a fresh portfolio project

Create a new repository from the class template. Call it `riis-portfolio`. Clone it, install dependencies, and start the dev server. You know the drill by now.

```bash
npm install
npm run dev
```

Open the project in Visual Studio Code and visit [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173). You'll see the article-style demo page our template ships with — the same one we've been recycling all semester.

A portfolio is not an article. It's something else entirely. So before we start adding new things, we need to clear away the old.

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Delete every component used in the article body — `ArticleHeader`, `ArticleBody`, `Image`, `Blockquote`, `BigNumber`, anything else the template inlines as demo content. Remove their imports from the script block at the top. Leave the file with just an empty script block and an empty body.

Your homepage should now look something like this:

```svelte
<script>
</script>
```

That's the right starting point. Save and check your browser. The page will be blank. We're going to fill it with something better.

## Part 3: Writing the content in YAML

A portfolio homepage has two parts that matter: who you are, and what you've made. Before we write any Svelte code, we're going to write all of the content first — in a separate data file that our components will read from.

In a real newsroom, content and code live in separate places. The reporter writing the words shouldn't have to touch a Svelte file. The designer changing the layout shouldn't risk breaking a sentence. The page-building system reads the content from one source and the design from another, and the two evolve independently.

We're going to mirror that pattern. Every word that appears on the page — the name, the tagline, the now/next paragraph, the project titles, the case studies — will live in a single content file. The Svelte code stays focused on rendering.

We've used JSON for content in past weeks. Today we'll try something different: [YAML](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YAML).

### Meeting YAML

You've actually seen YAML before. Back in Week 2, the GitHub Actions workflow file we activated was YAML. It's popular for content because it's easier to read and write than JSON — no curly braces, no commas at the end of every line, and a clean way to handle long prose.

Here's the same data in YAML versus JSON:

```yaml
title: How the Other Half Lives
date: 1890
skills:
  - Flash photography
  - Documentary writing
  - Lantern-slide lecturing
```

```json
{
  "title": "How the Other Half Lives",
  "date": 1890,
  "skills": ["Flash photography", "Documentary writing", "Lantern-slide lecturing"]
}
```

Indentation matters in YAML. Two spaces is the standard — never mix in tabs. Fields look like `key: value`. Lists use dashes. That's most of what you need to know.

### Multiline strings

The real reason we're switching to YAML is its handling of long prose. Writing multi-paragraph text inside a JSON string is miserable — every line break is a `\n\n`, every quote has to be escaped, nothing is readable.

YAML solves this with a feature called [block scalars](https://yaml-multiline.info/). Put a `|` after the key, indent the content, and write whatever you want:

```yaml
nowNext: |
  This is the first paragraph. It can be as long as you want.

  This is the second paragraph. Blank lines separate paragraphs,
  just like they would in a text file.
```

The `|` tells YAML to preserve line breaks as written. We'll use this for the now/next paragraph and for the case-study prose on each project page.

### Installing the YAML plugin

SvelteKit doesn't know how to read YAML out of the box. We'll add a Vite plugin called `@modyfi/vite-plugin-yaml` that teaches it how. Once the plugin is installed, you can import a `.yaml` file the same way you'd import JSON — Vite parses it at build time and hands you a ready-to-use JavaScript object.

Stop your dev server with `Ctrl + C` and run:

```bash
npm install @modyfi/vite-plugin-yaml
```

Then open `vite.config.js` and register the plugin:

```js {emphasize-lines="2,7"}
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import yaml from '@modyfi/vite-plugin-yaml';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    yaml(),
    sveltekit()
  ]
});
```

Restart with `npm run dev`.

### Writing the content file

Create a new folder at `src/lib/data/`. Inside it, make a file called `site.yaml`. This will be the single source of truth for everything that appears on your portfolio.

It has two top-level sections: `profile` and `projects`. We'll fill them in one at a time.

Start with the profile. Find a public-domain portrait of Riis on the [Library of Congress website](https://www.loc.gov/) or [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/), save it to `static/profile.jpg`, and paste this into `site.yaml`:

```yaml
profile:
  name: Jacob Riis
  tagline: Police reporter and tenement chronicler for the New-York Tribune and Evening Sun, documenting how the other half lives.
  photo: /profile.jpg
  photoAlt: Jacob Riis, in a portrait taken around 1900.
  nowNext: |
    I'm a police reporter at Mulberry Street headquarters for the Evening Sun, lecturing each week with lantern slides drawn from the flash photography I've been making in the Lower East Side.

    I'm seeking the chance to turn this work into reform — better housing laws, better schools for the children of the poor, and the demolition of Mulberry Bend.
```

A few things worth noticing.

I've left out the `email`, `github`, and `linkedin` fields entirely. Riis didn't have any of those. The Profile component handles missing fields gracefully because every contact link is wrapped in an `{#if}` block — write your component to expect any field to be missing, and the page renders cleanly. We'll use the same pattern on the project page in a moment.

The tagline is a single sentence, sixteen words, that tells you what Riis did and where. It echoes his most famous book without quoting it directly, which gives the line some pull.

### A note on the now/next paragraph

The `nowNext` field is the most important text on the homepage. It's a two-sentence pattern that career advisors call "now / next."

The first sentence says what the subject is doing now: their program, their focus, their current role. The second sentence says what they're seeking next: the kind of work they're looking for, where they'd like to do it, in what area.

Compare a vague version to a specific one:

- *Vague.* "I'm a journalism student interested in storytelling. I'm looking for opportunities to grow."
- *Specific.* "I'm a graduate student at CUNY focusing on data-driven housing reporting in New York. I'm looking for a summer internship at a newsroom investigating tenant displacement."

The specific version gives an editor or recruiter something concrete to evaluate. The vague version gives them nothing. Specificity is generosity.

The Riis now/next paragraph is written as if he were filling in his own portfolio around 1890. First-person voice, present-tense, period-specific. When you build a portfolio for a real journalist of the past, picking a moment in their career and writing from inside it makes the page feel alive in a way that a museum-plaque biography doesn't.

Now add the projects. Three of Riis's major works:

```yaml
projects:
  - slug: police
    title: Police Headquarters Reporter
    image: /thumbnails/police.jpg
    imageAlt: Mulberry Street police headquarters in the late 1880s
    liveUrl: https://www.loc.gov/collections/jacob-riis/
    summary: A decade of police-beat reporting at 300 Mulberry Street for the New-York Tribune and the Evening Sun, covering crime, immigration, and the daily life of the Lower East Side.
    skills:
      - Late-night police reporting
      - Source-building across language barriers
      - Working with the city coroner's records
      - Daily news pace
    meaningful: |
      The police beat is where I learned the city. Mulberry Street headquarters sits on the edge of the worst tenements in New York, and every story I covered as a reporter — every arrest, every fire, every dead body — pulled me deeper into the lives of the people on the other side of the door.

      I didn't yet know I would write a book about what I was seeing. But I was learning the geography, the families, and the patterns. The reform work that came later would have been impossible without the reporting work that came first.

  - slug: how-the-other-half
    title: How the Other Half Lives
    image: /thumbnails/bandits-roost.jpg
    imageAlt: Bandit's Roost, a Riis photograph of an alley off Mulberry Street, 1888
    liveUrl: https://www.loc.gov/item/06017839/
    summary: A book-length investigation of New York tenement life, illustrated with my own flash photographs of the city after dark. Published by Charles Scribner's Sons in 1890.
    skills:
      - Magnesium-flash photography
      - Long-form documentary writing
      - Lantern-slide lecturing
      - Reporting across class lines
    meaningful: |
      The flash powder gave me the picture, but the picture is only half the work. The other half is the words — telling readers in the brownstones uptown what the inside of a Five Points tenement smells like, sounds like, who lives there and how they came to be there.

      The book sold well enough that even Theodore Roosevelt came to find me. He left a note on my door saying he had read the book and had come to help. That was the moment I understood what reporting could do.

  - slug: mulberry-bend
    title: The Battle for Mulberry Bend
    image: /thumbnails/mulberry-bend.jpg
    imageAlt: Mulberry Bend before demolition, photographed in the early 1890s
    liveUrl: https://www.mcny.org/
    summary: A sustained reporting and advocacy effort to demolish Mulberry Bend, the most notorious slum block in lower Manhattan, and replace it with a public park. The block came down in 1894. Columbus Park stands there today.
    skills:
      - Sustained-focus accountability reporting
      - Working with city government and reform organizations
      - Combining photography, writing, and public lectures
      - Patience
    meaningful: |
      The Bend was three acres of the foulest tenement block in the city — narrow alleys where the sun never reached, buildings that should have been condemned a generation earlier, and a death rate among the children many times higher than the city average.

      I wrote about it for years. I photographed it. I lectured on it. Reform is slow. The Bend went from a block I first reported on as a young man to a public park nearly twenty years later.
```

Read through the YAML. Notice how the multiline `nowNext` and `meaningful` fields render cleanly in your editor — no escaped quotes, no `\n\n`, just paragraphs separated by blank lines. That's the payoff for using YAML.

While you're here, save thumbnail images to `static/thumbnails/police.jpg`, `bandits-roost.jpg`, and `mulberry-bend.jpg` to match the paths above. Riis's photographs are public domain and widely available — try the [Library of Congress Jacob Riis collection](https://www.loc.gov/collections/jacob-riis/), the [Museum of the City of New York](https://collections.mcny.org/), or [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Photographs_by_Jacob_Riis).

## Part 4: Building the homepage

Now we need to load this YAML and render it. The homepage has two visual parts: a Profile block for the personal branding, and a grid of Card tiles for the projects. Both are prefab components in the class template.

The Profile is brand new. I've added a component to `src/lib/components/` called `Profile.svelte`. It handles the personal-branding block: name, tagline, photo, contact links, and the now/next paragraph.

The Card component you've seen before. The class template includes it along with a `card-grid` layout class — together they handle the project tiles and the responsive grid that holds them. We've used both in earlier weeks.

### Loading the data

We'll use the `+page.js` load function pattern we established in Week 5. Create a new file at `src/routes/+page.js`:

```js
import site from '$lib/data/site.yaml';

export const load = () => {
  return site;
};
```

Thanks to the YAML plugin we installed earlier, the import reads `site.yaml` and hands us a JavaScript object — already parsed, no extra step needed. Because our YAML has top-level keys `profile` and `projects`, that's the shape the page receives. The page can read `data.profile` and `data.projects` without any extra unpacking.

### Rendering the page

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Replace the empty file with this:

```svelte
<script>
  import Profile from '$lib/components/Profile.svelte';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<Profile {...data.profile} />

<div class="card-grid">
  {#each data.projects as project}
    <Card
      href="{base}/projects/{project.slug}"
      image={project.image}
      imageAlt={project.imageAlt}
    >
      <h3>{project.title}</h3>
      <p>{project.summary}</p>
    </Card>
  {/each}
</div>
```

The `{...data.profile}` syntax is Svelte's prop-spreading shorthand. Instead of writing `name={data.profile.name} tagline={data.profile.tagline}` and so on for every field, we just spread the whole object. The Profile component pulls out the props it knows about and ignores the rest.

Save and check your browser. Riis should appear on the page — name as headline, tagline below, portrait photo, and the three project cards in a grid using his actual photographs.

<Screenshot
