  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That's the homepage done. Every word that shows up on it lives in `site.yaml`. Want to change the tagline? Edit one line of YAML. Want to add a fourth project? Add a fourth entry to the projects list. The Svelte code stays put.

Now we need a place for each card to lead.

## Part 5: Project detail pages

Every card on the homepage links to `/projects/[slug]` — a URL that doesn't exist yet. Click any of them and you'll get a 404. Let's fix that.

You've seen this pattern before. Back in [Week 9](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-9), when we built the Bronx Housing Violations database explorer, every building had its own page at `/building/[id]`. We didn't create a separate folder for each building. We created one dynamic route — a folder named `[id]` in square brackets — and SvelteKit matched any URL with the right shape and ran a load function to find the matching record.

We're going to do the same thing here, but with `[slug]` and YAML.

### The folder structure

Create the following folder structure inside `src/routes/`:

```
src/routes/
  +page.svelte           ← your homepage (already exists)
  +page.js               ← homepage load function (already exists)
  +layout.svelte         ← site shell (already exists)
  projects/
    [slug]/
      +page.svelte       ← project detail page (new)
      +page.js           ← detail load function (new)
```

The square brackets in `[slug]` tell SvelteKit this is a dynamic route. Any URL of the shape `/projects/anything` will be handled by the files inside.

### The load function

Create `src/routes/projects/[slug]/+page.js`:

```js
import site from '$lib/data/site.yaml';
import { error } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const load = ({ params }) => {
  const project = site.projects.find((p) => p.slug === params.slug);

  if (!project) {
    error(404, 'Project not found');
  }

  return { project };
};
```

This does three things:

1. Parses the YAML into a site object.
2. Finds the record inside `site.projects` whose `slug` matches the URL parameter. The `params` object is populated by SvelteKit based on the folder name — `[slug]` becomes `params.slug`.
3. If no record matches, throws a 404. Otherwise, hands the project record to the page.

Notice we're reading the same YAML file the homepage loads from. One source of truth for the whole site.

## Part 6: Designing the project page

We have the data flowing into the page. Now we need to render it.

Like the homepage, we'll use a prefab component to do the heavy lifting and keep the route file thin.

Create `src/lib/components/ProjectPage.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  let { project } = $props();

  let meaningfulParagraphs = $derived(
    project.meaningful ? project.meaningful.trim().split('\n\n') : []
  );
</script>

<article class="project">
  <a href="{base}/" class="back">← Back to all work</a>

  <h1>{project.title}</h1>

  {#if project.image}
    <img class="hero" src={project.image} alt={project.imageAlt} />
  {/if}

  {#if project.summary}
    <p class="summary">{project.summary}</p>
  {/if}

  {#if project.skills && project.skills.length > 0}
    <h2>What it took</h2>
    <ul class="skills">
      {#each project.skills as skill}
        <li>{skill}</li>
      {/each}
    </ul>
  {/if}

  {#if meaningfulParagraphs.length > 0}
    <h2>Why it mattered</h2>
    {#each meaningfulParagraphs as para}
      <p>{para}</p>
    {/each}
  {/if}

  <div class="cta">
    {#if project.liveUrl}
      <a href={project.liveUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="button primary">
        View the work →
      </a>
    {/if}
    {#if project.repoUrl}
      <a href={project.repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="button">
        View the code →
      </a>
    {/if}
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  .back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back:hover {
    color: var(--color-black);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: 2rem;
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  .hero {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  .summary {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .skills {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .skills li {
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    padding: 0.25rem 0.625rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.625rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
    color: var(--color-black);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .button:hover {
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  .button.primary {
    background: var(--color-accent);
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
  }

  .button.primary:hover {
    opacity: 0.9;
    background: var(--color-accent);
  }
</style>
```

It's a long component, but it's doing something simple: take one project record and lay out its parts. Every field is wrapped in an `{#if}` block, so a project missing an optional field — no `repoUrl` because there isn't a code repository for an 1890 book, say — still renders cleanly.

The "← Back to all work" link at the top is the page's navigation. There's nowhere else to go on this site, so a single back-link is the whole nav we need.

The `$derived()` call splits the multiline `meaningful` prose on blank lines into an array of paragraphs. This is the payoff for using YAML block scalars: prose written naturally in the data file renders as proper paragraphs on the page.

### Wiring up the route

All that's left is a thin wrapper file that puts the data and the template together. Open `src/routes/projects/[slug]/+page.svelte` and paste in:

```svelte
<script>
  import ProjectPage from '$lib/components/ProjectPage.svelte';
  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<ProjectPage project={data.project} />
```

That's the entire file. The load function fetches the data, the template component renders it, and the wrapper just connects them.

Save and click into one of the project cards on your homepage. You should see a full project page.

