## Part 7: Deploy and beyond

When you're ready, commit and push to GitHub. Watch the Actions tab to make sure the build succeeds.

Once your site is live, share the URL in the class chat. Pull up a few of your classmates' Riis sites. See how differently everyone treated the same source material.

### A note on custom domains

By default, your portfolio lives at `https://yourusername.github.io/riis-portfolio/`. That's a perfectly fine URL. But when you build out your own portfolio in the homework, you might want to point a domain you own — `yourname.com`, `yourname.press`, `yournameisajournalist.nyc` — at GitHub Pages and have it serve your site instead.

The process involves buying a domain from a registrar (Namecheap, Porkbun, and Cloudflare are all reasonable), updating its DNS records to point at GitHub's servers, and adding the domain in your repository's Pages settings. GitHub maintains a [step-by-step guide](https://docs.github.com/en/pages/configuring-a-custom-domain-for-your-github-pages-site) that walks through it.

I'll demo the configuration live in class, since DNS is fiddly enough that watching it once is more useful than reading it.

You don't have to do this. A `github.io` URL is professional. But if you've ever wanted to own your name on the web, this is the easiest way to start.

## Homework

Tonight you built Riis's portfolio with me. Now you'll build one of your own.

### Task 1: Pick your subject

You have two options.

**Option A: Build it for yourself.** Replace Riis with you. Edit `site.yaml` so the profile section holds your real name, tagline, photo, contact info, and now/next paragraph, and the projects section holds three of your own projects — your three Module 2 builds are the obvious choice, but you can include outside work if you have it. The result should be something you'd actually send to an editor.

**Option B: Build it for a different historical journalist.** Pick a real reporter, columnist, or correspondent from the past and build their portfolio the way we built Riis's. Some candidates: Ida B. Wells, Ida Tarbell, Lincoln Steffens, Nellie Bly, Dorothy Thompson, Ernie Pyle, Willa Cather, John Reed, Gay Talese, William F. Buckley. Pick three of their major works, write the YAML, and find public-domain images to illustrate.

Whichever option you pick, the technical requirements are the same: every field on every project filled in, every Profile field populated where appropriate, every link working, the site deployed to GitHub Pages.

### Task 2: Make it look like the subject

The default styles are fine, but they're generic. Spend real time making the site feel like it belongs to its subject — whether that's you, Riis if you want to keep going, or whoever you picked for Option B.

Some directions to play with:

- **Typography.** Swap the default font for something with personality. [Google Fonts](https://fonts.google.com/) is the easiest place to browse. A serif display face with a clean sans for body text is a classic editorial pairing. Riis's site might call for a 19th-century slab serif. Yours might call for something else entirely.
- **Color.** Pick a palette that fits the subject. Update the CSS variables in `src/app.css` and watch the whole site shift.
- **Layout.** The Profile photo could go to the side instead of below the name. The project grid could be one column instead of three. The back-link could be replaced with a more prominent nav.
- **Detail.** A subtle hover state on the cards. A custom favicon. A footer with a fake masthead.

This is a good place to lean on your AI coding assistant. Try prompts like "Change the color scheme to feel like a 19th-century newspaper" or "Make the project cards feel more like magazine covers." Iterate until the site feels intentional.

### Task 3: Prepare to present

Be ready to walk the class through your portfolio. You should be prepared to:

- Show the site on a projector or shared screen
- Explain one design choice you made and why
- Tell us who the site belongs to and what that decision changed about your approach
- Tell us what you'd add or change if you had another week
